From d83df19607eee33b536a4023e2cda8225f52a861 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mocoy <judsonwebdesign@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 18:12:50 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: give the hw-initiated CTL reset a realistic wait
 at resume

X1E80100 (Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x), v7.2 with pd_ignore_unused and
clk_ignore_unused dropped: the first suspend/resume campaign on this
config wedges the display after a few cycles. At resume the
hardware-initiated CTL reset is still in flight when the first atomic
commit polls for completion. dpu_hw_ctl_wait_reset_status() only polls
for DPU_REG_RESET_TIMEOUT_US (2 ms), a budget sized for the synchronous
driver-issued reset; on this platform the reset completes shortly after
the poll gives up (a devcoredump captured in the failure path shows
CTL_SW_RESET already cleared, with CTL_INTF_ACTIVE=0 while intf5 was
still scanning the panel).

The failure path then permanently unregisters the vblank IRQ callback,
so every later atomic commit times out forever (vblank timeout: 218c0,
enc42 frame done timeout) and the screen wedges until reboot.

Give the hw-initiated reset wait its own, wider budget (50 ms; only
paid when the bit is actually set) and leave the vblank IRQ registered
on failure so recovery is possible.

Reproduced deterministically in three suspend cycles on
fertile-forge (2026-09-16); evidence: kernel journal + devcoredump
snapshot of the failing commit.
---
 dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c |  9 +++++++--
 dpu_hw_ctl.c           | 12 +++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c b/dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c
index ba810f2..0f19fbf 100644
--- a/dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c
+++ b/dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c
@@ -571,8 +571,13 @@ static void dpu_encoder_phys_vid_prepare_for_kickoff(
 		DPU_ERROR_VIDENC(phys_enc, "ctl %d reset failure: %d\n",
 				ctl->idx, rc);
 		msm_disp_snapshot_state(drm_enc->dev);
-		dpu_core_irq_unregister_callback(phys_enc->dpu_kms,
-				phys_enc->irq[INTR_IDX_VSYNC]);
+		/*
+		 * Leave the vblank IRQ registered. Unregistering it here turned a
+		 * transient reset race at resume into a permanent display wedge:
+		 * the reset completes shortly after the failed wait, but with the
+		 * callback gone every later commit times out forever. A genuinely
+		 * stuck commit fails on its own timeout.
+		 */
 	}
 }
 
diff --git a/dpu_hw_ctl.c b/dpu_hw_ctl.c
index 36a497f..332f9cb 100644
--- a/dpu_hw_ctl.c
+++ b/dpu_hw_ctl.c
@@ -51,6 +51,16 @@
 #define CTL_FLUSH_MASK_CTL              BIT(17)
 
 #define DPU_REG_RESET_TIMEOUT_US        2000
+/*
+ * The hardware-initiated CTL reset (CTL_SW_RESET latched by hardware, e.g.
+ * when the mdss power domain cycles during system suspend) completes
+ * asynchronously. At resume the display clock domain can still be ramping
+ * when the first atomic commit polls for its completion, so the wait needs
+ * a wider budget than the synchronous driver-issued reset: on X1E80100 the
+ * reset was still in progress after the 2 ms poll but had completed by the
+ * time the failure path captured a devcoredump snapshot moments later.
+ */
+#define DPU_HW_RESET_WAIT_TIMEOUT_US    50000
 #define  MERGE_3D_IDX   23
 #define  DSC_IDX        22
 #define CDM_IDX         26
@@ -454,7 +464,7 @@ static int dpu_hw_ctl_wait_reset_status(struct dpu_hw_ctl *ctx)
 		return 0;
 
 	pr_debug("hw ctl reset is set for ctl:%d\n", ctx->idx);
-	if (dpu_hw_ctl_poll_reset_status(ctx, DPU_REG_RESET_TIMEOUT_US)) {
+	if (dpu_hw_ctl_poll_reset_status(ctx, DPU_HW_RESET_WAIT_TIMEOUT_US)) {
 		pr_err("hw recovery is not complete for ctl:%d\n", ctx->idx);
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
-- 
2.55.0

